WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:03.630
- [Narrator] Lumber has
never been more expensive.

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:06.850
Two-by-fours are more than
three times their typical price.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:08.870
Other wood products that
we use to build houses,

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:10.850
like plywood, have also soared.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:14.210
The increases are thanks to
an unexpected boom in demand.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.040
Even in the broad rally that has pushed up

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.120
commodity prices across the board,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.790
from crude oil to corn,

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.310
the dramatic climb in
wood prices stands out.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.090
Lumber futures have more than tripled

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.700
since the rally began in April 2020.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.020
No one expected wood prices to soar,

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.220
not even saw mills, lumber dealers,

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:33.900
and others who profit from rising prices.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.400
Here's how lumber prices climbed

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.170
to records during the
pandemic, who has profited,

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.880
and why those who grow the trees

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.393
haven't benefited at all.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:44.640
When the pandemic set in,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.490
saw mills shut down like
many other businesses.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.090
Before the lockdown, it was shaping up

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.390
to be a strong home building season.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.390
But when the global economy
ground to a halt in 2020,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.600
new housing construction
in the U.S. plummeted.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.080
- At the onset of the pandemic,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.820
lumber mills shut down,

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.180
dealers liquidated their inventories,

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.800
and speculators started
shorting lumber futures,

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.820
expecting prices to plunge.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.390
No one thought that there
was a housing boom coming

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:11.840
or a remodeling boom.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.360
- [Narrator] Hunkered-down
homeowners remodeled in mass,

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.460
and low mortgage rates drove
demand for suburban housing,

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.170
while restaurants raced to construct

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.090
outdoor dining accommodations.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.870
Lumber and plywood started
flying off shelves.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.710
The building boom lifted the share price

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.100
of home improvement giants
Lowe's and Home Depot

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.020
ahead of the broader market.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.790
Wood was in short supply.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.770
Sawmills ramped up capacity
but couldn't catch up,

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.950
leading to a surge in prices.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.660
- Besides prices rising, demand is such

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.160
that some people can't
even get the right amounts

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.980
or the right products of lumber.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.740
You're seeing this throughout,

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.670
across products, from a
two-by-four, to plywood,

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.260
to engineered products for home builders.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.640
- [Narrator] Sawmills have
been big beneficiaries.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.810
Owners of Southern mills
have seen profits skyrocket,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.080
and their share prices have surged.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.020
Farther up the wood supply chain,

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.840
those who grow the trees that become

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.270
the two-by-fours have been left out.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.600
Timber growers supply mills with logs,

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.460
which are sawed into lumber
and mashed into pulp.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.570
Prices for logs and lumber
usually move in unison,

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:16.970
but lately their prices have diverged.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:20.050
Much of the nation's logs are
harvested in the U.S. South,

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:21.820
and Southern growers have gained nothing

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.630
from the run-up in prices
for finished lumber.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.810
Earl and Wanda Barrs
have been growing timber

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.150
for 35 years in Central Georgia.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:30.540
They say prices for their timber

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.950
have not kept pace with
the run-up in lumber.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.730
- The landowner, us, we haven't benefited

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.530
from the increase in the price of lumber.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.433
- When I was a procurement forester,

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.340
we were paying

00:02:43.653 --> 00:02:46.490
$25 a ton for sawed timber

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.657
on the stump, 40 years ago.

00:02:50.502 --> 00:02:52.420
If you bring that forward today,

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.040
the market is $25 or
less on the stump today,

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.930
and that's in constant dollars.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.590
That's not taking into account inflation

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.550
and other things that have gone up

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.870
to grow those trees.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.450
- [Narrator] The problem is
that there's so many trees.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.530
Between 1980 and 2020,

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:10.780
the volume of wood per acre of timberland

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.800
in many Southern counties
has more than quadrupled.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.920
- In the 1980s, the
federal government paid

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.730
a lot of landowners in
the South to plant trees.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.640
The government wanted to take
crop land out of rotation

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.970
and to stop having a lot
of corn and soybeans,

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.440
so the prices would rise in those markets.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.850
They paid people to grow trees

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.240
because those trees would
sit on the land for decades

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.340
and just grow, and accumulate value,

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.810
and eventually be ready to be made

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.060
into things like lumber and plywood.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:36.893
- [Narrator] Decades later,

00:03:36.893 --> 00:03:38.860
many of those trees are being harvested

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.800
which has flooded the market
with an abundance of timber.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.410
- There's so many trees in fact,

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.250
that mills can basically name their price

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.470
for the logs that they take
in and saw into boards.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:50.560
The supply and demand is so out of whack

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.660
that analysts and a lot of
investors in sawmill companies

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.430
think it could be a decade or two

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:57.350
before enough trees are cut down

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.170
that supply and demand is back in balance,

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.700
even with the increased demand

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.680
we're seeing today for wood products.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.910
- [Narrator] Earl Barr says
the consolidation of the market

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.370
has also made it difficult to sell timber.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:11.170
- 20 years ago, if we'd
have had a track of timber

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:12.620
that we would've put up for bids,

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.240
we'd have gotten at least
10 independent bids,

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.770
competitive bids, for that track of wood.

00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:22.800
Now, we're seeing fewer
than 5, if you're lucky.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.830
We're not looking at what
is happening to our markets.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.310
Are we playing on a level playing field?

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.283
Are we able to put our trees

00:04:32.283 --> 00:04:35.590
into a truly competitive marketplace?

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:37.260
- [Narrator] Analysts
do expect lumber prices

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.960
will eventually go down,

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.880
but with the housing market
continuing to run red hot,

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.093
the price continues to rise.

